Polymorphism means “many forms”.It means defining one entity with multiple behaviours
-> In java we have following 2 types of poly morphisms.
1. Compile time polymorphism
2. Runtime Polymorphism

| Overloading           | Overriding           |
| --------------------- | -------------------- |
| Compile-time          | Runtime              |
| Same class            | Parent-child         |
| Static binding        | Dynamic binding      |
| No inheritance needed | Inheritance required |

In Java, polymorphism allows:
  Same method call
  To behave differently
  Based on the actual object at runtime

Polymorphism allows one interface or parent reference to represent multiple object forms.
Polymorphism depends on inheritance or interfaces

| Benefit             | Explanation                                  |
| ------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| Loose coupling      | Code depends on abstraction                  |
| Extensibility       | New behavior without modifying existing code |
| Maintainability     | Removes large if-else blocks                 |
| Runtime flexibility | Behavior decided at runtime                  |
| Core of Spring      | DI, AOP, Proxies                             |

3.1 Compile-Time Polymorphism
✔ Achieved using method overloading

3.2 Runtime Polymorphism
✔ Achieved using method overriding + inheritance/interface

4. Compile-Time Polymorphism (Method Overloading)
Definition

Multiple methods with:
Same name
Different parameter list (type / count / order)

class Calculator {

    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}

Key Points:
  Resolved at compile time
  Return type alone cannot differentiate methods
  Can overload:
    static methods
    constructors
  Does not depend on inheritance

❌ Invalid:
int add(int a, int b) {}
double add(int a, int b) {} // ❌ return type only


5. Runtime Polymorphism (Most Important Topic)
Definition
  Method call is resolved at runtime based on object type, not reference type.

class Parent {
    void show() {
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    @Override
    void show() {
        System.out.println("Child");
    }
}

Parent p = new Child();
p.show();  // Child

👉 Rule to remember

Reference decides accessibility, object decides behavior.

6. Conditions for Runtime Polymorphism
  ✔ IS-A relationship (inheritance / interface)
  ✔ Method overriding
  ✔ Parent reference
  ✔ Child object
  ❌ static methods
  ❌ final methods
  ❌ private methods

7. How Runtime Polymorphism Works (JVM Internals 🔥)
    Compiler checks method existence using reference type
    JVM resolves method call using actual object
    Uses Dynamic Method Dispatch
    Internally uses Virtual Method Table (V-Table)
👉 That’s why overridden methods are called at runtime.

8. Method Overriding Rules (Very Important)
| Rule             | Explanation              |
| ---------------- | ------------------------ |
| Method signature | Must be same             |
| Access modifier  | Cannot reduce visibility |
| Return type      | Covariant allowed        |
| static           | Cannot override          |
| final            | Cannot override          |
| private          | Not inherited            |

9. Static Methods & Polymorphism (Tricky Interview Area)
class Parent {
    static void show() {
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    static void show() {
        System.out.println("Child");
    }
}

Parent p = new Child();
p.show();   // Parent
  👉 This is method hiding, NOT polymorphism
  👉 Static binding happens at compile time

10. Polymorphism Using Interfaces (Very Important)
interface Payment {
    void pay();
}

class CreditCardPayment implements Payment {
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Credit Card");
    }
}

class UpiPayment implements Payment {
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("UPI");
    }
}

